Flock Finder data rights and source terms
=========================================

Status reviewed: 2026-10-14

This file records the source terms that apply to WiGLE-derived records. It does
not grant new rights, replace WiGLE's terms, or provide legal advice.

Source and access record
------------------------

Source: WiGLE wireless network database
Source URL: https://wigle.net/
API endpoint used: https://api.wigle.net/api/v2
Data access method: authenticated WiGLE API queries

Official terms reviewed:

1. WiGLE Individual, Non-Commercial Data License Agreement
   URL: https://wigle.net/eula.html
   Accessed: 2026-10-14
   Page states: Updated 7/12/18

2. WiGLE Frequently Asked Questions, license and commercial-use sections
   URL: https://wigle.net/faq#commercial
   Accessed: 2026-10-14

Rights confirmed by the reviewed sources
-----------------------------------------

The individual agreement grants a non-exclusive right to use WiGLE maps and
the access-point database for personal, research, or educational noncommercial
purposes. It reserves rights that the agreement does not expressly grant.

The FAQ says WiGLE does not want its data used for unlicensed commercial gain.
It also says commercial licensing is suspended at the time of this review.

Limits and unresolved rights
----------------------------

The reviewed terms do not identify the WiGLE database as open data. The MIT
license in this repository applies to project code, not to WiGLE-derived
records.

The individual agreement restricts copying, transfer, distribution, and sale
of data for commercial consideration, gain, or benefit. It also bars sharing
account credentials or using an account to give a third party API access.

A free and unsponsored personal or research site aligns with the agreement's
listed purposes. Free access alone does not settle every right. Publishing a
searchable map, downloadable files, or release snapshots gives third parties
copies of WiGLE-derived records. The reviewed pages do not expressly answer
whether this form of public, noncommercial redistribution is permitted.

The owner states that Flock Finder is a free, unsponsored, personal-use
website. The repository and deployed pages contained store links, a discount
code, and product promotion when reviewed on 2026-10-14. Those features do not
by themselves prove sponsorship, affiliate compensation, or commercial gain.
The reviewed terms do not answer whether uncompensated product promotion
affects WiGLE's classification of this use.

Written clarification or qualified legal review is still needed for these
questions:

1. Does WiGLE permit public display of the filtered records and their precise
   coordinates on a free, noncommercial site?
2. Does WiGLE permit downloadable GeoJSON, CSV, PMTiles, vector tiles, or other
   snapshots derived from its API results?
3. Does filtering, classifying, deduplicating, or combining records make a
   derived dataset whose redistribution terms differ from the source data?
4. Does uncompensated product promotion or use of a discount code affect
   WiGLE's classification of a free, unsponsored, personal-use project?
5. Does WiGLE require specific attribution, removal handling, access controls,
   or retention limits for a public project?
6. Must immutable snapshots be deleted if the individual license terminates?

No answer or permission has been inferred for these questions.

Attribution record
------------------

Use this factual attribution where WiGLE-derived records are displayed:

    Data source: WiGLE (https://wigle.net/). Accessed through the WiGLE API.
    WiGLE does not endorse Flock Finder. Source terms apply.

The reviewed individual agreement does not state a standalone attribution
condition. Attribution does not expand the project's rights to use or
redistribute the data.

Operational rules
-----------------

1. Do not label WiGLE-derived records as MIT-licensed, public-domain, or open
   data.
2. Do not treat public availability or API access as redistribution permission.
3. Do not publish WiGLE credentials, tokens, account access, or raw API
   responses containing secrets.
4. Preserve the source URL, access date, source commit, scan window, record
   counts, schemas, and hashes with each snapshot.
5. Recheck the official terms before a material change in use, funding,
   distribution, or access model.
